"untitled (https://home.engineering.iastate.edu/~morris/papers/10/ieeetc10.pdf)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 10; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://home.engineering.iastate.edu/~morris/papers/10/ieeetc10.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 927\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTight Bounds for Memory Allocation With and Without Request Fragmentation (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.28462v1)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 30; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.28462v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1657\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmain.dvi (https://cse.buffalo.edu/~mhertz/gcmalloc-oopsla-2005.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":185}); Total lines: 1295\nL71@P0: It is possible to measure the costs of garbage collection activity\nL72@P0: (e.g., tracing and copying) [10, 20, 30, 36, 56] but it is impossi\u0002ble to subtract garbage collection’s effect on mutator performance.\nL73@P0: Garbage collection alters application behavior both by visiting and\nL74@P0: reorganizing memory. It also degrades locality, especially when\nL75@P0: physical memory is scarce [61]. Subtracting the costs of garbage\nL76@P0: collection also ignores the improved locality that explicit memory\nL77@P0-1: managers can provide by immediately recycling just-freed mem\u0002ory [53, 55, 57, 58]. For all these reasons, the costs of precise,(a) Step one: collection and derivation of death records. (b) Step two: execution with explicit memory management.\nL78@P1: Figure 1: The oracular memory management framework.\nL79@P1: copying garbage collection versus explicit memory management\nL80@P1: have never been quantified.\nL81@P1: Contributions\nL82@P1: In this paper, we conduct an empirical comparison of garbage col\u0002lection to explicit memory management in Java. To enable this\nL83@P1: comparison, we develop an “oracular” memory manager. This mem\u0002ory manager relies on an oracle that indicates when the system\nL84@P1: should deallocate objects (i.e., by calling free on them). During\nL85@P1: a profiling run, the system gathers object lifetimes and generates a\nL86@P1: program heap trace that is later processed to generate the oracles.\nL87@P1: We use two different oracles that span the range of possible explicit\nL88@P1: deallocation calls. The lifetime-based oracle is the most aggressive:\nL89@P1: it uses object lifetimes to instruct the memory manager to free ob\u0002jects after their last use — the earliest time they can safely be freed.\nL90@P1: The reachability-based oracle is the most conservative: it reclaims\nL91@P1: objects at the last moment a program could call free (i.e., when\nL92@P1: they become unreachable). The reachability-based oracle relies on\nL93@P1: precise object reachability information obtained by processing the\nL94@P1: program heap traces with the Merlin algorithm [33, 34]. We discuss\nL95@P1: these two approaches in detail in Section 3.\nL96@P1: We find that an on-line version of the reachability-based oracle\nL97@P1: interferes with mutator locality and increases runtime from 2%–\nL98@P1: 33%. We eliminate this problem by executing the oracular memory\nL99@P1: manager inside an extended version of Dynamic SimpleScalar, an\nL100@P1: architecturally-detailed simulator [15, 39]. This approach allows\nL101@P1: us to measure the cost of Java execution and memory management\nL102@P1: operations while excluding disruptions caused by consulting the\nL103@P1: oracle. We believe this framework is of independent interest for\nL104@P1: studying memory management policies.\nL105@P1: We use this framework to measure the impact of garbage collec\u0002tion versus explicit memory management on runtime performance,\nL106@P1: space consumption, and page-level locality. We perform these mea\u0002surements across a range of benchmarks, garbage collectors (in\u0002cluding copying and non-copying collectors), and explicit memory\nL107@P1: managers.\nL108@P1: We find that GenMS, an Appel-style generational collector us\u0002ing a mark-sweep mature space, provides runtime performance that\nL109@P1: matches that provided by the best explicit memory manager when\nL110@P1: given five times as much memory, occasionally outperforming it by\nL111@P1: up to 9%. With three times as much memory, its performance lags\nL112@P1: so that it performs an average of 17% slower. Garbage collection\nL113@P1: performance degrades further at smaller heap sizes, ultimately run\u0002ning an average of 70% slower. Explicit memory management also\nL114@P1: exhibits better memory utilization and page-level locality, gener\u0002ally requiring half or fewer pages to run with the same number of\nL115@P1: page faults and running orders-of-magnitude faster when physical\nL116@P1: memory is scarce.\nL117@P1: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2\nL118@P1: presents the oracular memory management framework in detail,\nL119@P1: and Section 3 discusses its implications and limitations. Sections 4\nL120@P1: and 5 present experimental methodology and results comparing ex\u0002plicit memory management to a range of different garbage collec\u0002tors. Section 6 addresses related work, Section 7 discusses future\nL121@P1: directions, and Section 8 concludes.\nL122@P1: 2. Oracular Memory Management\nL123@P1: Figure 1 presents an overview of the oracular memory management\nL124@P1: framework. As Figure 1(a) shows, it first executes the Java pro\u0002gram to calculate object lifetimes and generate the program heap\nL125@P1: trace. The system processes the program heap trace uses the Mer\u0002lin algorithm to compute object reachability times and generate\nL126@P1: the reachability-based oracle. The lifetime-based oracle comes di\u0002rectly from the lifetimes computed during the profiling run. Using\nL127@P1: these oracles, the oracular memory manager executes the program\nL128@P1: as shown in Figure 1(b), allocating objects using calls to malloc\nL129@P1: and invoking free on objects when directed by the oracle (see\nL130@P1: Figure 2). Because trace generation takes place inside the simu\u0002lator and oracle generation happens off-line, the system measures\nL131@P1: only the costs of allocation and deallocation.\nL132@P1: Below we describe these steps in detail and discuss our solutions\nL133@P1: to the challenges of generating our oracles, detecting memory allo\u0002cation operations, and inserting explicit deallocation calls without\nL134@P1: distorting program execution. We discuss the implications and lim\u0002itations of our approach in Section 3.\nL135@P1: 2.1 Step One: Data Collection and Processing\nL136@P1: For its Java platform, the oracular memory manager uses an ex\u0002tended version of Jikes RVM version 2.3.2 configured to produce\nL137@P1: PowerPC Linux code [2, 3]. Jikes is a widely-used research plat\u0002form written almost entirely in Java. A key advantage of Jikes and\nL138@P1: freed by unreachable\nL139@P1: reachability-based oracle\nL140@P1: freed by\nL141@P1: live lifetime-based oracle dead\nL142@P1: reachable\nL143@P1: can be explicitly freed\nL144@P1: can be collected\nL145@P1: Figure 2: Object lifetime. The lifetime-based oracle frees ob\u0002jects after their last use (the earliest safe point), while the\nL146@P1-2: reachability-based oracle frees them after they become un\u0002reachable (the last possible moment an explicit memory man\u0002ager could free them).its accompanying Memory Management Toolkit (MMTk) is that it\nL147@P2: allows us to use a number of garbage collection algorithms [11].\nL148@P2: The oracular memory manager executes inside Dynamic Simple\u0002Scalar (DSS) [39], an extension of the SimpleScalar superscalar\nL149@P2: architectural simulator [15] that permits the use of dynamically\u0002generated code.\nL150@P2: Repeatable Runs\nL151@P2: Because the oracular memory manager uses allocation order to\nL152@P2: identify objects, we must ensure that the sequence of allocations\nL153@P2: is identical from run to run. We take a number of steps in Jikes\nL154@P2: RVM and the simulator to ensure repeatable runs. We use the\nL155@P2: “fast” configuration of Jikes RVM, which optimizes as much of\nL156@P2: the system as possible and compiles it into a prebuilt virtual ma\u0002chine. Jikes RVM uses timer-based sampling at runtime to optimize\nL157@P2: methods once they reach a certain “hotness” level. To eliminate\nL158@P2: this considerable source of nondeterminism, we use a pseudoadap\u0002tive methodology [38, 49], which optimizes only “hot” methods,\nL159@P2: as determined from the mean of 5 runs. We also employ deter\u0002ministic thread switching, which switches threads based upon the\nL160@P2: number of methods executed rather than at regular time intervals.\nL161@P2: Finally, we modify DSS to update the simulated OS time and reg\u0002ister clocks deterministically. Rather than use cycle or instruction\nL162@P2: counts, which will change when we add calls to free, our modi\u0002fications advance these clocks a fixed amount at each system call.\nL163@P2: These changes ensure that all runs are perfectly repeatable.\nL164@P2: Tracing for the Liveness-Based Oracle\nL165@P2: During a profiling run, the simulator calculates object lifetimes and\nL166@P2: generates program heap traces for later use. The simulator obtains\nL167@P2: per-object lifetime information by recording the location of every\nL168@P2: allocated object. At each memory access, the simulator then looks\nL169@P2: up the object being used and updates its latest lifetime (in alloca\u0002tion time). Unfortunately, this does not capture every use of an\nL170@P2: object. For example, testing for equality is a use of both argu\u0002ment objects, but Java’s equality operation compares addresses and\nL171@P2: does not examine memory locations. To capture these object uses,\nL172@P2: we also mark all root-referenced objects as being in use. This ex\u0002tended definition potentially overestimates object lifetimes slightly,\nL173@P2: but eliminates the risk of freeing an object too early.\nL174@P2: The system also preserves objects that we feel a programmer\nL175@P2: could not reasonably free. For instance, while our system can de\u0002tect the last use of code and type information, these objects are not\nL176@P2: something that a developer would be able to deallocate in a real\nL177@P2: program. Similarly, our system will not free objects used to opti\u0002mize class loading. These optimizations include objects mapping\nL178@P2: class member, string names, and type information in class files to\nL179@P2: their Jikes internal representation. Other objects that a programmer\nL180@P2: would not free and we therefore preserve enable lazy method com\u0002pilation and reduce the time spent scanning jar files. At the end of\nL181@P2: the profiling run, the system preserves all of these objects and those\nL182@P2: to which these objects refer by extending their lifetime to the end\nL183@P2: of the program.\nL184@P2: Tracing for the Reachability-Based Oracle\nL185@P2: To generate the reachability-based oracle, we compute object reach\u0002ability information efficiently and precisely using the Merlin algo\u0002rithm [33, 34]. Our off-line implementation of the Merlin algo\u0002rithm operates by analyzing a program heap trace. During this\nL186@P2: trace processing, the Merlin algorithm updates a timestamp asso\u0002ciated with an object whenever it might become unreachable, e.g.,\nL187@P2: when a pointer to it is overwritten. Once the entire trace has been\nL188@P2: processed, it generates “death records”, a list ordered by allocation\nL189@P2: time that indicates which objects become unreachable at that time.\nL190@P2: A key difficulty is capturing all the information required by the\nL191@P2: Merlin algorithm without affecting program execution. We need\nL192@P2: to execute the same code during step one (trace collection) as we\nL193@P2: do during step two (simulation). Otherwise, memory allocation\nL194@P2: and object lifetimes will be different, as the compiler will generate\nL195@P2: differently-sized chunks of code. However, there is a significant\nL196@P2: amount of information that the Merlin algorithm requires, includ\u0002ing all object allocations, intra-heap pointer updates, and program\nL197@P2: roots whenever an object could be freed. It is not possible to obtain\nL198@P2: this information from the normal optimized code.\nL199@P2: Replacing normal opcodes with illegal ones is at the heart of\nL200@P2: our approach for non-intrusively generating the needed heap traces.\nL201@P2: The new opcodes uniquely identify key events such as when new\nL202@P2: objects are allocated. When the simulator encounters such an op\u0002code, it outputs a record into the trace. It then executes the illegal\nL203@P2: opcode exactly like its legal variant.\nL204@P2: To enable the generation of these illegal opcodes, we extend\nL205@P2: Jikes RVM’s compiler intermediate representations. Jikes RVM in\u0002cludes nodes in its IRs to differentiate between method calls within\nL206@P2: the VM and calls to the host system, minimizing the modifications\nL207@P2: needed to support different OS calling conventions. We build upon\nL208@P2: these by adding a set of nodes to represent calls to malloc. This\nL209@P2: extension allows the compiler to treat object allocations like any\nL210@P2: other function call, while emitting an illegal opcode instead of the\nL211@P2: usual branch instruction. We also modify Jikes RVM to replace\nL212@P2: intra-heap reference stores with illegal instructions. These opcodes\nL213@P2: allow us to detect events needed for heap tracing without inserting\nL214@P2: code that would distort instruction cache behavior.\nL215@P2: 2.2 Step Two: Simulating Explicit Memory Management\nL216@P2: Before each allocation, the simulator consults the oracle to deter\u0002mine if any objects should be freed. When freeing an object, it\nL217@P2: saves the function parameter (the size request for malloc) and\nL218@P2: jumps to free instead, but sets the return address so that execution\nL219@P2: returns to the malloc call rather than the following instruction.\nL220@P2: The simulator repeats this cycle until there are no objects left to be\nL221@P2: reclaimed, and then allocation and program execution continues as\nL222@P2: normal. Both malloc and free are invoked via method calls.\nL223@P2: When these functions are implemented outside of the VM, they are\nL224@P2: called using the Jikes foreign function interface (VM SysCall);\nL225@P2: we discuss the impact of this in Section 3.2.\nL226@P2: 2.3 Validation: Live Oracular Memory Management\nL227@P2: In addition to the simulation-based framework described above,\nL228@P2: we implemented a “live” version of the oracular memory manager\nL229@P2: which uses the reachability-based oracle but actually runs on a real\nL230@P2: machine. Like the simulation-based oracular memory manager, the\nL231@P2: live oracle executes Java programs, but uses the actual instructions\nL232@P2: in place of the illegal ones. This live oracular memory manager\nL233@P2: uses the object lifetime information and a buffer recording where\nL234@P2: objects are allocated to fill a special “oracle buffer” containing the\nL235@P2: addresses of the objects to be freed. To determine the program’s\nL236@P2: running time, we measure the total execution time and then sub\u0002tract the time spent checking if objects should be freed and time\nL237@P2: spent refilling the buffer containing the addresses of the objects to\nL238@P2: free.\nL239@P2: To measure the distortion introduced by the oracle, we compare\nL240@P2: the cost of running garbage collection as usual to running with a\nL241@P2: null oracle. The null oracle loads the buffers in the same way as\nL242@P2: the real oracular memory manager, but otherwise execution pro\u0002ceeds normally (it does not actually free any objects). We found\nL243@P2: that the distortion introduced is unacceptably large and erratic. For\nL244@P2: example, with the GenMS collector, the 228 jack benchmark with\nL245@P2-3: the null oracle reports a 12% to 33% increase in runtime versusrunning with no oracle. By contrast, the null oracle slows the same\nL246@P3: collector down by at most 3% when running the 213 javac bench\u0002mark. Other collectors also show distortions from the null oracle,\nL247@P3: but without any obvious or predictable patterns. We attribute these\nL248@P3: distortions to pollution of both the L1 and L2 caches induced by\nL249@P3: processing the oracle buffers.\nL250@P3: While the live oracular memory manager is too noisy to be re\u0002liable for precise measurements, its results lend credence to the\nL251@P3: simulation-based approach. As Figure 5 shows, the live version\nL252@P3: closely mirrors the trends of the reachability oracle simulation re\u0002sults.\nL253@P3: 3. Discussion\nL254@P3: In the preceding sections, we have focused on the methodology we\nL255@P3: employ, which strives to eliminate measurement noise and distor\u0002tion. Here we discuss some of the key assumptions of our approach\nL256@P3: and address possible concerns. These include invoking free on\nL257@P3: unreachable and dead objects, the cost of using foreign function\nL258@P3: calls for memory operations, the effect of multithreaded environ\u0002ments, unmeasured costs of explicit memory management, the role\nL259@P3: of custom memory allocators, and the effects of memory managers\nL260@P3: on program structure. While our methodology may appear to hurt\nL261@P3: explicit memory management (i.e., making garbage collection look\nL262@P3: better), we argue that the differences are negligible.\nL263@P3: 3.1 Reachability versus Liveness\nL264@P3: The oracular memory manager analyzes explicit memory manage\u0002ment performance using two very different oracles. The liveness\u0002based oracle deallocates objects aggressively, invoking free at the\nL265@P3: first possible opportunity it can safely do so. The reachability ora\u0002cle instead frees objects at the last possible moment in the program\nL266@P3: execution, since calls to free require a reachable pointer as a pa\u0002rameter.\nL267@P3: As described in Section 2.1, the liveness-based oracle preserves\nL268@P3: some objects beyond their last use. The liveness-based oracle also\nL269@P3: frees some objects that the reachability oracle does not. The num\u0002ber of objects involved is small: only pseudoJBB at 3.8% and\nL270@P3: 201 compress at 4.4% free more than 0.8% more objects. The\nL271@P3: liveness-based oracle makes these additional calls to free only for\nL272@P3: objects that do not become unreachable but which plausibly could\nL273@P3: be deallocated by a knowledgeable programmer.\nL274@P3: Real program behavior is likely to fall between these two ex\u0002tremes. We would expect few programmers to reclaim objects im\u0002mediately after their last use, and similarly, we would not expect\nL275@P3: them to wait until the very last point objects are reachable before\nL276@P3: freeing them. These two oracles thus bracket the range of explicit\nL277@P3: memory management options.\nL278@P3: We show in Section 5.1 that the gap between these two oracles\nL279@P3: is small. Both oracles provide similar runtime performance, while\nL280@P3: the liveness oracle reduces heap footprints by at most 15% over\nL281@P3: the reachability oracle. These results generally coincide with pre\u0002vious studies of both C and Java programs. Hirzel et al. compare\nL282@P3: liveness to reachability on a benchmark suite including seven C ap\u0002plications [37]. For these, they find that when using an aggressive,\nL283@P3: interprocedural liveness analysis, they find a significant gap for two\nL284@P3: of their benchmarks, reducing average object lifetime by 11% for\nL285@P3: gzip (in allocation time) and 21% for yacr2); for the others, the\nL286@P3: gap remains below 2%. In a study including five of the benchmarks\nL287@P3: we examine here, Shaham et al. measure the average impact of in\u0002serting null assignments in Java code, simulating nearly-perfect\nL288@P3: placement of explicit deallocation calls [51]. They report an av\u0002erage difference in space consumption of 15% over deallocating\nL289@P3: objects when they become unreachable.\nL290@P3: 3.2 malloc Overhead\nL291@P3: When using allocators implemented in C, the oracular memory\nL292@P3: manager invokes allocation and deallocation functions through the\nL293@P3: Jikes VM SysCall foreign function call interface. While not free,\nL294@P3: these calls do not incur as much overhead as JNI invocations. Their\nL295@P3: total cost is just 11 instructions: six loads and stores, three register\u0002to-register moves, one load-immediate, and one jump. This cost\nL296@P3: is similar to that of invoking memory operations in C and C++,\nL297@P3: where malloc and free are functions defined in an external li\u0002brary (e.g., libc.so).\nL298@P3: We also examine an allocator which implements malloc and\nL299@P3: free within the Jikes RVM. In this case, the oracular memory\nL300@P3: manager uses the normal Jikes RVM method call interface rather\nL301@P3: than the VM SysCall interface. Because we still need to deter\u0002mine when an allocation occurs and, where appropriate, insert calls\nL302@P3: to free, we still cannot inline the allocation fast path. While this\nL303@P3: may prevent some potential optimizations, we are not aware of any\nL304@P3: explicitly-managed programming language that implements mem\u0002ory operations without function call overhead.\nL305@P3: 3.3 Multithreaded versus Single-threaded\nL306@P3: In the experiments we present here, we assume a single-processor\nL307@P3: environment and disable atomic operations both for Jikes RVM and\nL308@P3: for the Lea allocator. In a multithreaded environment, most thread\u0002safe memory allocators also require at least one atomic operation\nL309@P3: for every call to malloc and free: a test-and-set operation for\nL310@P3: lock-based allocators, or a compare-and-swap operation for non\u0002blocking allocators [46]. These atomic operations are very costly\nL311@P3: on some architectures. For example, on the Pentium 4, the cost\nL312@P3: of the atomic CMPXCHG operation (compare-and-swap) is around\nL313@P3: 124 cycles. Because garbage collection can amortize the cost of\nL314@P3: atomic operations by performing batch allocations and dealloca\u0002tions, Boehm observes that it can be much faster than explicit mem\u0002ory allocation [13].\nL315@P3: However, the issue of multithreaded versus single-threaded en\u0002vironments is orthogonal to the comparison of garbage collectors\nL316@P3: and explicit memory managers, because explicit memory alloca\u0002tors can also avoid atomic operations for most memory operations.\nL317@P3: In particular, a recent version of Hoard [6] (version 3.2) main\u0002tains thread-local freelists, and generally uses atomic operations\nL318@P3: only when flushing or refilling them. Use of these thread-local\nL319@P3: freelists is cheap, normally through a register reserved for access\u0002ing thread-local variables. On architectures lacking such support,\nL320@P3: Hoard places the freelists at the start of each thread stack (aligned\nL321@P3: on 1MB boundaries), and accesses them by bitmasking a stack vari\u0002able.\nL322@P3: 3.4 Smart Pointers\nL323@P3: Explicit memory management can have other performance costs.\nL324@P3: For example, C++ programs might manage object ownership by\nL325@P3: using smart pointers. These templated classes transparently im\u0002plement reference counting, which would add expense to every\nL326@P3: pointer update. For example, on the gc-bench benchmark, the per\u0002formance of the Boost “intrusive pointer” that embeds reference\u0002counting within an existing class is up to twice as slow as the\nL327@P3: Boehm-Demers-Weiser collector. 1\nL328@P3: However, smart pointers do not appear to be in widespread use.\nL329@P3: We searched for programs using the standard auto ptr class or\nL330@P3: the Boost library’s shared ptr [16] on the open-source web site\nL331@P3: sourceforge.net and found only two large programs that use\nL332@P3: them. We attribute this lack of use both to their cost, since C++\nL333@P3-4: 1Richard Jones, personal communication.Benchmark statistics\nL334@P4: Benchmark Total Alloc Max Reach Alloc/Max\nL335@P4: 201 compress 125,334,848 13,682,720 9.16\nL336@P4: 202 jess 313,221,144 8,695,360 36.02\nL337@P4: 205 raytrace 151,529,148 10,631,656 14.25\nL338@P4: 209 db 92,545,592 15,889,492 5.82\nL339@P4: 213 javac 261,659,784 16,085,920 16.27\nL340@P4: 228 jack 351,633,288 8,873,460 39.63\nL341@P4: ipsixql 214,494,468 8,996,136 23.84\nL342@P4: pseudoJBB 277,407,804 32,831,740 8.45\nL343@P4: Table 1: Memory usage statistics for our benchmark suite. To\u0002tal allocation and maximum reachable are given in bytes. Al\u0002loc/max denotes the ratio of total allocation to maximum reach\u0002able, and is a measure of allocation-intensiveness.\nL344@P4: programmers tend to be particularly conscious of expensive opera\u0002tions, and to their inflexibility. For example, the same smart pointer\nL345@P4: class cannot be used to manage scalars and arrays, because C++ ar\u0002rays require a different syntax for deletion (delete []).\nL346@P4: Instead, C and C++ programmers generally use one of the fol\u0002lowing conventions: a function caller either allocates objects that it\nL347@P4: then passes to its callee, or the callee allocates objects that it returns\nL348@P4: to its caller (as in strncpy). These conventions impose little to\nL349@P4: no performance overhead in optimized code.\nL350@P4: Nonetheless, some patterns of memory usage are inherently dif\u0002ficult to manage with malloc and free. For example, the al\u0002location patterns of parsers makes managing individual objects an\nL351@P4: unacceptably-difficult burden. In these situations, C and C++ pro\u0002grammers often resort to custom memory allocators.\nL352@P4: 3.5 Custom Allocation\nL353@P4: Many explicitly-managed programs use custom allocators rather\nL354@P4: than general-purpose allocators both to simplify and to accelerate\nL355@P4: memory management. In particular, Berger et al. show that region\u0002style allocation is both useful for a variety of workloads and can be\nL356@P4: much faster than general-purpose allocation, but that they gener\u0002ally consume much more space than needed [8]. Exploring custom\nL357@P4: allocation policies like regions is beyond the scope of this paper.\nL358@P4: 3.6 Program Structure\nL359@P4: The programs we examine here were written for a garbage-collected\nL360@P4: environment. Had they been written in a language with explicit\nL361@P4: memory management, they might have been written differently.\nL362@P4: Unfortunately, we do not see any way to quantify this effect. It\nL363@P4: would be possible (though onerous) to attempt to measure it by\nL364@P4: manually rewriting the benchmark applications to use explicit deal\u0002location, but we would somehow have to factor out the impact of\nL365@P4: individual programmer style.\nL366@P4: Despite the apparent difference in program structure that one\nL367@P4: might expect, we observe that it is common for Java programs to\nL368@P4: assign null to objects that are no longer in use. In this sense,\nL369@P4: programming in a garbage-collected environment is at least occa\u0002sionally analogous to explicit memory management. In particular,\nL370@P4: explicit nulling of pointers resembles the use of delete in C++,\nL371@P4: which then can trigger a chain of class-specific object destructors.\nL372@P4: 4. Experimental Methodology\nL373@P4: To quantify the performance of garbage collection versus explicit\nL374@P4: memory management, we compare the performance of eight bench\u0002marks across a variety of garbage collectors. Table 1 presents our\nL375@P4: benchmarks. We include most of the SPECjvm98 benchmarks [18].\nL376@P4: ipsixql is a persistent XML database system, and pseudoJBB is a\nL377@P4: Garbage collectors\nL378@P4: MarkSweep non-relocating, non-copying single-generation\nL379@P4: GenCopy two generations with copying mature space\nL380@P4: SemiSpace two-space single-generation\nL381@P4: GenMS two generations with non-copying mature space\nL382@P4: CopyMS nursery with whole-heap collection\nL383@P4: Allocators\nL384@P4: Lea combined quicklists and approximate best-fit\nL385@P4: MSExplicit MMTk’s MarkSweep with explicit freeing\nL386@P4: Table 2: Memory managers examined in this paper. Section 4\nL387@P4: presents a more detailed description of the allocators and col\u0002lectors.\nL388@P4: fixed-workload variant of the SPECjbb benchmark [17]. pseudo\u0002JBB executes a fixed number of transactions (70,000), which sim\u0002plifies performance comparisons.\nL389@P4: For each benchmark, we run each garbage collector with heap\nL390@P4: sizes ranging from the smallest in which they complete to four\nL391@P4: times larger. For our simulated runs, we use the memory and proces\u0002sor configuration of a PowerPC G5 processor [1], and assume a 2\nL392@P4: GHz clock. We use a 4K page size, as in Linux and Windows.\nL393@P4: Table 3 presents the exact architectural parameters.\nL394@P4: Rather than relying on reported heap usage, we compare actual\nL395@P4: heap footprints by examining each run’s maximum number of heap\nL396@P4: pages in use. Pages are “in use” only if they have been allocated\nL397@P4: from the kernel and touched. We do not include unused pages,\nL398@P4: such as allocated but untouched pages or pages that have been un\u0002mapped, since they are not assigned physical memory. Counting\nL399@P4: pages in use ensures the proper accounting of all memory usage,\nL400@P4: including metadata space, which is occasionally underreported.\nL401@P4: For the oracular memory management experiments, we use both\nL402@P4: the Lea (GNU libc, “DLmalloc”) allocator [44] and a variant of the\nL403@P4: MMTk MarkSweep collector. The Lea allocator is an approximate\nL404@P4: best-fit allocator that provides both high speed and low memory\nL405@P4: consumption. It forms the basis of the memory allocator included\nL406@P4: in the GNU C library [28]. The version used here (2.7.2) is a hy\u0002brid allocator with different behavior based on object size, although\nL407@P4: objects of different sizes may be adjacent in memory. Small ob\u0002jects (less than 64 bytes) are allocated using exact-size quicklists\nL408@P4: (one linked list of freed objects for each multiple of 8 bytes). The\nL409@P4: Lea allocator coalesces objects in these lists (combining adjacent\nL410@P4: free objects) in response to several conditions, such as requests for\nL411@P4: medium-sized objects. Medium objects are managed with imme\u0002diate coalescing and splitting of the memory on the quicklists and\nL412@P4: approximates best-fit. Large objects are allocated and freed us\u0002ing mmap. The Lea allocator is the best overall allocator (in terms\nL413@P4: of the combination of speed and memory usage) of which we are\nL414@P4: aware [40].\nL415@P4: While the Lea allocator is an excellent point of comparison, it\nL416@P4: differs significantly from the garbage collectors we examine here.\n"
